Chapter: Arm64Stdio
  cites Foreword chapter StringUtils

Section: Body

 The wasm transport half for arm64, the sibling of `Arm64Plug`'s serial one
 and of `RiscVStdio`. `Arm64Plug` hands the wire to `serial-write-via-buf`,
 which reaches a serial port that does not exist on this target.

 Two differences from the riscv shim, both from `Arm64Plug`'s own dispatch.
 `a64-emit-module` takes a fifth argument, the SMP flag, which the network
 entry reads out of its mode string with `text-contains mode "smp"`; there is
 no mode string here and a page building a phone binary is not asking for a
 multi-core kernel, so it is False. And the state constructor is
 `make-a64-state` rather than an empty record.

 `write-binary` takes the byte list itself, so unlike the serial path this
 needs no `a64-wire-length`: the length is the list's own. That is one fewer
 place for a derived count to disagree with the bytes it describes.

 An `ELF\n` prefix asks for a board kernel instead of the wire, the same mode
 line `RiscVStdio` reads. Without this the module ignored the mode and always
 answered the wire, which is the arm the page's board control exists to catch.

 `a64-record-func` stores `st.insn-count`, an INSTRUCTION INDEX, and
 `a64-build-elf` adds its argument to the load address as BYTES, so the index
 is scaled by 4 here. RISC-V records the same trap for the same reason: an
 unscaled index lands a fraction of the way to the right instruction, and on
 AArch64 every instruction is 4 bytes so it also lands unaligned.

  plug-emit-ir-bytes : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  plug-emit-ir-bytes (input) =
   let want-elf = text-starts-with input "ELF\n"
   in let ir-text = if want-elf then text-drop input 4 else input
   in let parsed = parse-ir-chapter ir-text
   in let cg-state = a64-emit-module make-a64-state (parsed.chapter) (parsed.type-defs) (parsed.effect-op-names) False
   in if want-elf then a64-emit-board-elf cg-state
   else write-binary (a64-build-wire-output cg-state)

  a64-emit-board-elf : A64State -> [Console] Nothing
  a64-emit-board-elf (st) =
   let idx = a64-find-func-offset (st.func-names) (st.func-offsets) "__start" 0
   in if idx < 0 then print-line-uni "REFUSED this unit has no `__start`, so a board kernel would have no runtime init to enter"
   else write-binary (a64-build-elf (__buf-read-bytes (st.code-buf) 0 (st.code-pos)) (st.data) (idx * 4))
